WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:24.070
It’s one of the greatest nations in the
world.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:28.260
Germany is the lynchpin of the European Union,
an economic powerhouse that’s also home

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:30.849
to one of the world’s top creative capitals.

00:00:30.849 --> 00:00:37.330
Despite a history scarred by two world wars
and 45 years of partition under a Communist

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:42.820
dictatorship, it’s a nation that’s consistently
been at the center of global affairs for 150

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:43.820
years.

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:48.260
And it only exists because of one man.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:53.140
When Otto von Bismarck was born, Germany was
a collection of 39 weak states cowering between

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:57.140
the superpowers of France, Austria, and Russia.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:02.230
By the time he died, the German nation had
been forged in blood and iron and Central

00:01:02.230 --> 00:01:04.809
Europe had a new sheriff in town.

00:01:04.809 --> 00:01:09.360
But while we all know the modern Germany he
gave us, few of us know much about Bismarck

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:10.360
himself.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:16.300
In this video, we take a look at the man known
to history as the Iron Chancellor.

00:01:16.300 --> 00:01:31.560
From the Ashes
When Otto von Bismarck was born in the Prussian

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:36.890
village of Schönhausen on April 1, 1815,
it was into a world that had been shattered

00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:38.000
by war.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.470
Not ten years before, Napoleon’s Grand Army
had come storming through Central Europe,

00:01:42.470 --> 00:01:46.470
destroying the Holy Roman Empire and leaving
the Germanic states in ruins.

00:01:46.470 --> 00:01:51.580
Bismarck’s own home of Prussia had been
deeply humiliated by a French military occupation.

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.540
Not that young Otto was aware of any of this.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:57.409
Less than three months after he was born,
Napoleon was defeated at Waterloo.

00:01:57.409 --> 00:02:02.080
For baby Otto, Napoleon’s defeat meant being
able to grow up on his parent’s estate without

00:02:02.080 --> 00:02:03.080
war.

00:02:03.080 --> 00:02:04.230
That’s right: estate.

00:02:04.230 --> 00:02:07.670
Despite the image he’d later cultivate as
a man of the people, Otto von Bismarck came

00:02:07.670 --> 00:02:09.479
from the Prussian nobility.

00:02:09.479 --> 00:02:14.800
His father was a Junker, a kind of minor aristocrat,
while his mother came from a family of middle

00:02:14.800 --> 00:02:15.910
class professionals.

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:18.860
Still, money doesn’t equal comfort.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:24.220
Aged 7, Otto’s strict mother sent him away
to school in the Prussian capital, Berlin,

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:26.300
an experience Otto absolutely hated.

00:02:26.300 --> 00:02:33.340
He hated it so much, in fact, that it gave
him a lifelong loathing of strong women like

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.340
his mother.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:37.900
While Otto was sulking in Berlin, Europe was
trying to rebuild itself after Napoleon.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:42.060
The destruction of the thousand year-old Holy
Roman Empire had left a void in the center

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:43.060
of the continent.

00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:46.930
To fill it, the surviving superpowers created
something called the German Confederation,

00:02:46.930 --> 00:02:51.440
a collection of 39 states locked in loose
association.

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:54.230
The Confederation was designed to be weak.

00:02:54.230 --> 00:02:58.720
Its power was balanced between the Catholic
Austrian Empire in the south, and the Protestant

00:02:58.720 --> 00:03:02.580
kingdom of Prussia – where Otto von Bismarck
was born – in the north.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.750
As we run through Bismarck’s life, it’s
important to remember there was nothing at

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:10.180
this stage that even remotely resembled modern
Germany.

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:15.130
When you hear us say Bismarck studied in Hanover,
it wasn’t like you moving from, say, Colorado

00:03:15.130 --> 00:03:16.180
to Washington.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:21.560
It was like literally moving to another country,
albeit one where they spoke the same language.

00:03:21.560 --> 00:03:23.830
This language thing is exactly why people
would soon start wondering why there were

00:03:23.830 --> 00:03:27.730
39 Germanic states instead of one united Germany.

00:03:27.730 --> 00:03:30.030
But more on that in a moment.

00:03:30.030 --> 00:03:35.900
For now all you need to know is Otto entered
Göttingen university in Hanover in the 1830s

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.290
and studied to be a lawyer.

00:03:38.290 --> 00:03:42.170
Remarkably for such a great man, he was useless
as a student.

00:03:42.170 --> 00:03:46.030
His friends considered him a dandy, and his
tutors considered him a drunk.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:50.920
He scraped through his course and joined the
Prussian civil service, a terminally boring

00:03:50.920 --> 00:03:51.920
occupation.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:56.040
In fact, it was so boring that Bismarck wrote:
“the Prussian civil servant is like one

00:03:56.040 --> 00:04:00.810
musician in an orchestra… he’s confined
to his own little part…

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.750
I, however, want to make my own music.”

00:04:03.750 --> 00:04:07.710
It’s a quote Bismarck fans are fond of,
because it points to his future greatness.

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:11.650
At the time though, that future greatness
was very much in doubt.

00:04:11.650 --> 00:04:15.730
Bismarck actually quit the civil service in
1839 after his mother died and returned to

00:04:15.730 --> 00:04:17.000
his father’s estate.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:23.139
There he briefly became a Bible-thumping evangelical,
before marrying the pious Johanna von Puttkamer

00:04:23.139 --> 00:04:25.909
and living as just another Prussian farmer.

00:04:25.909 --> 00:04:31.199
The one notable thing he did came in 1847,
when he was sent to the Prussian parliament

00:04:31.199 --> 00:04:34.030
and made a number of speeches attacking the
nation’s Jews.

00:04:34.030 --> 00:04:39.400
At this stage, in his early 30s, he really
was little more than a random hick from Pomerania.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.499
You know the saying, “commeth the hour,
commeth the man”?

00:04:42.499 --> 00:04:47.130
Well, no-one in 1847 Europe knew it, but the
hour was very nearly upon them.

00:04:47.130 --> 00:04:52.550
And the man was already here.

00:04:52.550 --> 00:04:54.599
Revolution!

00:04:54.599 --> 00:05:02.330
In the library of European history, 1848 is
the giant red volume simply titled “Whoa!”

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:07.809
On February 22, a French government ban against
banquets led to a Paris uprising which deposed

00:05:07.809 --> 00:05:08.809
the king.

00:05:08.809 --> 00:05:14.199
Barely three weeks later, on March 12, a similar
uprising in Vienna toppled Klemens von Metternich,

00:05:14.199 --> 00:05:18.279
the guy who’d been holding Europe together
since Napoleon’s defeat.

00:05:18.279 --> 00:05:21.909
It was a spread of revolution, a contagion
of uprisings.

00:05:21.909 --> 00:05:25.740
And, after Metternich fell, nowhere was safe.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:29.659
Within days, Berlin was paralyzed by riots
which nearly brought down the conservative

00:05:29.659 --> 00:05:32.750
Prussian king, Friedrich Wilhelm IV.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:38.099
It was only by promising a slew of liberal
reforms that Friedrich Wilhelm was able to

00:05:38.099 --> 00:05:39.740
cling onto power.

00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:41.199
One of those reforms?

00:05:41.199 --> 00:05:44.430
People could now discuss the possibility of
uniting Germany.

00:05:44.430 --> 00:05:48.669
For a guy who’s famous for uniting Germany,
you might be surprised to hear Otto von Bismarck

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:51.849
wanted no part of this nationalist revolution.

00:05:51.849 --> 00:05:55.699
While Liberals in Prussia and the other German
states were holding elections and creating

00:05:55.699 --> 00:06:00.879
a parliament to write a new, pan-German constitution,
Bismarck was considering arming his peasants

00:06:00.879 --> 00:06:04.899
and marching on Berlin to preserve Prussian
sovereignty.

00:06:04.899 --> 00:06:08.089
In the end, though, Bismarck settled for using
his position in the Prussian parliament to

00:06:08.089 --> 00:06:11.319
become one of the loudest anti-revolutionaries
in Europe.

00:06:11.319 --> 00:06:15.620
The thing you’ve gotta remember is that
“Germany” had never been a thing before.

00:06:15.620 --> 00:06:20.899
For Bismarck, uniting all 39 states in the
German Confederation meant turning his beloved,

00:06:20.899 --> 00:06:25.899
Protestant Prussia into a support player in
a nation dominated by the Catholic Austrians.

00:06:25.899 --> 00:06:30.199
On top of that, the guys now pushing to unite
Germany were all middle class Liberals, while

00:06:30.199 --> 00:06:33.559
Bismarck was a reactionary, ultra-conservative
aristocrat.

00:06:33.559 --> 00:06:36.520
He loved his king, he loved God, and he loved
order.

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:39.059
For Bismarck, 1848 must have been a living
hell.

00:06:39.059 --> 00:06:43.550
But that turbulent year did provide Bismarck
with one important boost.

00:06:43.550 --> 00:06:47.849
It brought Bismarck firmly to Friedrich Wilhelm
IV’s attention.

00:06:47.849 --> 00:06:52.619
Throughout the revolution, Bismarck was one
of the most pro-monarchy men in Prussia.

00:06:52.619 --> 00:06:57.620
Even when it seemed the old order was about
to be swept away he clung stubbornly to the

00:06:57.620 --> 00:07:01.080
past, like a reactionary German limpet.

00:07:01.080 --> 00:07:06.429
So when the forces of counterrevolution finally
got control in 1849, Bismarck was perfectly

00:07:06.429 --> 00:07:08.020
placed to reap the rewards.

00:07:08.020 --> 00:07:13.110
As the revolutions failed in Prussia, Austria,
and elsewhere, thousands of German Liberals

00:07:13.110 --> 00:07:14.919
were forced to flee to America.

00:07:14.919 --> 00:07:17.809
By 1851, the old order had been completely
restored.

00:07:17.809 --> 00:07:22.770
For his services to the Prussian crown, Bismarck
was made ambassador to the city state of Frankfurt.

00:07:22.770 --> 00:07:27.909
Of all the countries rocked by 1848, only
one underwent permanent change.

00:07:27.909 --> 00:07:31.559
In France, where it had all kicked off, the
old monarchist order was destroyed.

00:07:31.559 --> 00:07:36.020
In its place, a president rose up who’d
soon become an emperor.

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:41.069
His name was Napoleon III, and if you’ve
already seen our video on him, you’ll know

00:07:41.069 --> 00:07:49.729
he and Otto von Bismarck were destined to
collide with enough force to reshape the continent.

00:07:49.729 --> 00:07:57.749
Iron and Blood
On September 30, 1862, a giant of a man slowly

00:07:57.749 --> 00:08:03.139
lumbered to his feet in the Prussian parliament
and delivered a speech that would change European

00:08:03.139 --> 00:08:04.139
history.

00:08:04.139 --> 00:08:08.119
At 6ft4, and with a body built not unlike
a particular type of brick outhouse, Otto

00:08:08.119 --> 00:08:11.349
von Bismarck was a presence that always commanded
attention.

00:08:11.349 --> 00:08:17.039
But on this day, it was his words rather than
his size or volcanic temper that set everyone

00:08:17.039 --> 00:08:18.039
talking.

00:08:18.039 --> 00:08:22.219
In the 11 years since 1851, Bismarck had been
serving as Friedrich Wilhelm’s ambassador

00:08:22.219 --> 00:08:23.529
to the capitals of Europe.

00:08:23.529 --> 00:08:24.710
He’d been in Frankfurt.

00:08:24.710 --> 00:08:25.710
St Petersburg.

00:08:25.710 --> 00:08:29.709
He’d even briefly been stationed in the
court of Napoleon III in Paris.

00:08:29.709 --> 00:08:33.580
And being so close to the levers of power
had given him time to think.

00:08:33.580 --> 00:08:37.890
Slowly, Bismarck had come to realize that
the world was changing whether he liked it

00:08:37.890 --> 00:08:38.890
or not.

00:08:38.890 --> 00:08:42.870
Men’s dreams of a united Germany hadn’t
died with 1848.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:47.220
And the Catholic Austrians were ascendant
in the German Confederation even without a

00:08:47.220 --> 00:08:48.220
union.

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:51.540
Either the Prussian giant stood by and watched
the Liberals’ and Austrians’ dreams come

00:08:51.540 --> 00:08:54.520
true, or he beat them at their own game.

00:08:54.520 --> 00:08:59.260
It was from these musings that Bismarck’s
famous “Blood and Iron” speech sprang.

00:08:59.260 --> 00:09:03.480
On that day, September 30, 1862, Bismarck
was in a precarious position.

00:09:03.480 --> 00:09:07.940
Roughly a year before, Friedrich Wilhelm IV
had died, and the Prussian crown had passed

00:09:07.940 --> 00:09:11.720
to his dull, dimwitted son Wilhelm I.

00:09:11.720 --> 00:09:16.500
Before 12 months had passed, Wilhelm had accidentally
sparked a crisis that nearly toppled his government.

00:09:16.500 --> 00:09:20.290
The short version is he needed tax rises that
the Liberal-dominated Prussian parliament

00:09:20.290 --> 00:09:22.280
simply wasn’t willing to pass.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:26.490
With it looking like another revolution might
be on the cards, Wilhelm panicked and recalled

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:32.110
his father’s old acolyte Bismarck from Paris
to become Prussia’s Minister-President.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:33.990
No-one expected Bismarck to last eight weeks.

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:38.460
And now here he was, about to deliver his
first major speech to a hostile parliament.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:41.750
No-one could have predicted what a bombshell
it would be.

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:46.640
In his booming, Germanic voice, Bismarck declared:
"The position of Prussia in Germany will not

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:50.950
be determined by its liberalism but by its
power.”

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:54.260
He went on to say:
“Not through speeches and majority decisions

00:09:54.260 --> 00:10:01.000
will the great questions of the day be decided—that
was the great mistake of 1848 and 1849—but

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:03.000
by iron and blood.”

00:10:03.000 --> 00:10:09.160
For the first time here, we see Bismarck explicitly
addressing the revolution of 1848 not as something

00:10:09.160 --> 00:10:14.710
horrific to be forgotten, but as something
that was just done wrong.

00:10:14.710 --> 00:10:19.070
Parliament had tried talking and building
consensus and all it had done was make chaos.

00:10:19.070 --> 00:10:25.800
Now it was time to do it right, to unify Germany
with force and iron and blood.

00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:28.170
To be clear, iron didn’t just mean war.

00:10:28.170 --> 00:10:30.350
It meant rapid industrialization.

00:10:30.350 --> 00:10:35.340
It meant a Prussia that was building ships
and bridges and railways so fast it’d catch

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:37.310
up with Britain and France.

00:10:37.310 --> 00:10:39.880
But, yes, it did also mean war.

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:44.080
Nobody in the parliament knew it, but Bismarck
was about embark on three great wars that

00:10:44.080 --> 00:10:48.470
would take Germany from 39 shattered states
to a single behemoth under Prussian control.

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:53.230
For the Liberals in Parliament, “iron and
blood” was the last thing they wanted to

00:10:53.230 --> 00:10:54.230
hear.

00:10:54.230 --> 00:10:58.410
They tried to force Wilhelm I to remove his
new minister-president from power, even refusing

00:10:58.410 --> 00:11:02.220
to work with him when Bismarck began collecting
new taxes without their approval.

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:03.720
Not that this bothered Bismarck.

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:08.180
He’d returned to Prussia with a plan to
forge Germany in his conservative, protestant

00:11:08.180 --> 00:11:09.180
image.

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:12.270
And the wheels – forged from the strongest
iron, naturally – were already in motion.

00:11:12.270 --> 00:11:13.450
War
The British Prime Minister Lord Palmerston

00:11:13.450 --> 00:11:14.980
once remarked:
“Only three people have ever really understood

00:11:14.980 --> 00:11:24.580
the Schleswig-Holstein business—the Prince
Consort, who is dead—a German professor,

00:11:24.580 --> 00:11:56.820
who has gone mad—and I, who have forgotten
all about it.”

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:16.070
All

00:12:16.070 --> 00:12:57.230
of which is our way of saying you don’t
need to worry about the super-complicated

00:12:57.230 --> 00:12:59.510
background to the Second Schleswig War.

00:12:59.510 --> 00:13:03.970
All you need to know is that Schleswig and
Holstein were two regions on the border between

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:09.520
the German Confederation and Denmark, and
that on February 1, 1864, Bismarck convinced

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:12.290
the Austrians to join him in invading them.

00:13:12.290 --> 00:13:17.190
The Second Schleswig War is today seen as
the beginning of the German Wars of Unification.

00:13:17.190 --> 00:13:19.790
More or less everything went according to
Bismarck’s plan.

00:13:19.790 --> 00:13:23.940
Prussia and Austria stomped Demark, occupied
the peninsula, and within months had taken

00:13:23.940 --> 00:13:26.150
control of Schleswig-Holstein.

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:29.230
Holstein went to Austria, while Schleswig
went to Prussia.

00:13:29.230 --> 00:13:32.810
In the 21st Century, it’s fashionable for
historians to debate whether Bismarck really

00:13:32.810 --> 00:13:35.920
was a genius, or just extraordinarily lucky.

00:13:35.920 --> 00:13:40.500
It’s certainly true that the Second Schleswig
War was possible only thanks to a convenient

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:42.279
succession crisis in Schleswig-Holstein.

00:13:42.279 --> 00:13:43.290
But the rest of it?

00:13:43.290 --> 00:13:47.180
Austria had no beef with Schleswig-Holstein,
they didn’t want to invade.

00:13:47.180 --> 00:13:51.160
But Bismarck managed to convince Vienna that
not joining his war would make Prussia too

00:13:51.160 --> 00:13:53.600
powerful in the German Confederation.

00:13:53.600 --> 00:13:58.490
So join it Austria did, a move that cost them
most of their allies – just as Bismarck

00:13:58.490 --> 00:13:59.490
wanted.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:03.460
With Austria diplomatically isolated, Bismarck
then checked in on his old frenemy, Napoleon

00:14:03.460 --> 00:14:07.100
III, to ask if Paris had any desire to protect
Vienna.

00:14:07.100 --> 00:14:12.470
When Napoleon III was all like “nah, that’s
cool dude,” Bismarck set about engineering

00:14:12.470 --> 00:14:14.180
his second major war.

00:14:14.180 --> 00:14:18.730
In January, 1866, Bismarck found a pretext
in Austria’s administration of Holstein.

00:14:18.730 --> 00:14:23.760
Again, the ins and outs don’t matter; what
matters is that, by June, Austria and several

00:14:23.760 --> 00:14:28.810
German states, including Hannover, were ready
for war with Prussia.

00:14:28.810 --> 00:14:31.180
This was an exceptionally bad move.

00:14:31.180 --> 00:14:35.220
The moment the starting gun fired on the Seven
Weeks’ War, Prussia’s ultra-modern, ultra-efficient

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:40.350
army was bulldozing its way across Central
Europe, crushing everything in its path.

00:14:40.350 --> 00:14:45.490
Seven weeks after the conflict exploded, the
Austrian army had been utterly defeated.

00:14:45.490 --> 00:14:48.010
Back in Berlin, Kaiser Wilhelm couldn’t
believe his luck.

00:14:48.010 --> 00:14:52.279
He and his general staff could already envisage
Prussian troops marching through Vienna.

00:14:52.279 --> 00:14:53.279
But that never happened.

00:14:53.279 --> 00:14:55.360
Bismarck put his extremely heavy foot down.

00:14:55.360 --> 00:14:59.840
There would be no march on Vienna, no humiliating
peace treaty for Austria.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:05.140
All he wanted was to force Vienna to promise
they’d never, ever join a united Germany.

00:15:05.140 --> 00:15:09.660
If they agreed, then any future Germany would
naturally be dominated not by the Catholic

00:15:09.660 --> 00:15:12.820
Habsburgs, but by the protestant Prussians.

00:15:12.820 --> 00:15:16.070
This practically gave Wilhelm a stroke.

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:20.780
The Kaiser was so adamant Austria be crushed
that Bismarck had to threaten to throw himself

00:15:20.780 --> 00:15:25.000
from a fourth floor window if Wilhelm gave
the order to march on Vienna.

00:15:25.000 --> 00:15:27.470
In the end, Bismarck’s theatrics carried
the day.

00:15:27.470 --> 00:15:31.990
The Austrians agreed to leave this whole uniting
Germany thing to Prussia, and Vienna was left

00:15:31.990 --> 00:15:32.990
standing.

00:15:32.990 --> 00:15:35.589
Back in Berlin, the two wars had made Bismarck
a hero.

00:15:35.589 --> 00:15:38.690
Even the Liberals who’d tried to get him
fired now sang his praises.

00:15:38.690 --> 00:15:43.300
Their chorus only got louder when Bismarck
annexed Schleswig and Holstein and Hanover,

00:15:43.300 --> 00:15:48.850
and forced every Germanic state north of the
River Main into his new North German Confederation.

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:54.840
By July, 1867, those 39 Germanic states we
started this video with had been reduced to

00:15:54.840 --> 00:15:56.100
just five.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:16:00.650
In the south, there were Bavaria, Baden, Württemberg,
and Hesse-Darmstadt.

00:16:00.650 --> 00:16:04.860
In the north, Bismarck’s new, Prussian-dominated
North German superstate straddled the Baltic

00:16:04.860 --> 00:16:05.860
Sea.

00:16:05.860 --> 00:16:09.760
All that was needed now was some pretext for
getting the southern states onboard this new

00:16:09.760 --> 00:16:11.330
thing called Germany.

00:16:11.330 --> 00:16:18.370
Luckily, Bismarck had plans for one final
war.

00:16:18.370 --> 00:16:23.910
Death of an Empire
For most in Europe, the creation of the North

00:16:23.910 --> 00:16:26.040
German Confederation was greeted with enthusiasm.

00:16:26.040 --> 00:16:30.330
For Liberals and German nationalists, it was
the moment they’d been waiting for since

00:16:30.330 --> 00:16:31.330
1848.

00:16:31.330 --> 00:16:35.440
For the major powers, it was a useful corrective
to the void left by the Holy Roman Empire’s

00:16:35.440 --> 00:16:36.440
destruction.

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:40.360
But there was one man who was very displeased
with the way things were going.

00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:42.940
In Paris, Napoleon III stewed in bitterness.

00:16:42.940 --> 00:16:46.650
Having given Bismarck his blessing to go to
war with Austria, the French Emperor had been

00:16:46.650 --> 00:16:51.089
expecting something in return, and made moves
to annex Luxembourg for France.

00:16:51.089 --> 00:16:52.660
But Bismarck stopped him.

00:16:52.660 --> 00:16:56.610
Worse, he threatened to go to war over Luxembourg’s
neutrality.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:17:00.610
Suddenly France was looking like the weak
European power against this booming new nation

00:17:00.610 --> 00:17:01.610
of Germany.

00:17:01.610 --> 00:17:05.970
So when a succession crisis blew up in Spain
in 1870 and Prussia tried to step in, Napoleon

00:17:05.970 --> 00:17:08.220
III decided to assert his empire.

00:17:08.220 --> 00:17:12.139
As you’ll know if you’ve watched our video
on Napoleon III – and trust us, these two

00:17:12.139 --> 00:17:17.429
videos really complement one another – Paris
dispatched the French ambassador to Ems, where

00:17:17.429 --> 00:17:20.669
he accosted a vacationing Kaiser Wilhelm I.

00:17:20.669 --> 00:17:23.800
The two had an argument and that was that.

00:17:23.800 --> 00:17:25.319
Only it wasn’t quite.

00:17:25.319 --> 00:17:28.590
See, Bismarck somehow got hold of a memo about
that argument.

00:17:28.590 --> 00:17:33.769
By now a master of the dark arts of politics,
he subtly edited what had been said so it

00:17:33.769 --> 00:17:37.460
appeared French honor had been unforgivably
insulted.

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:41.690
Known as the Ems Dispatch, Bismarck’s edited
memo did it’s job perfectly.

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:46.549
When Bismarck leaked its contents to the press,
the French public was so outraged that Napoleon

00:17:46.549 --> 00:17:47.910
III declared war.

00:17:47.910 --> 00:17:51.950
For the previous three years, Bismarck had
been trying to cajole the southern Germanic

00:17:51.950 --> 00:17:55.029
states into joining his new confederation
without success.

00:17:55.029 --> 00:17:59.260
But with the French suddenly preparing to
invade Prussia through those southern states,

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:02.360
the holdout German rulers changed their tune.

00:18:02.360 --> 00:18:06.899
Terrified of a dollar store Napoleon rampaging
through their backyards, Bavaria and the rest

00:18:06.899 --> 00:18:09.070
fled into Bismarck’s embrace.

00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:10.429
Bismarck made it simple.

00:18:10.429 --> 00:18:13.340
His army would protect them from the French,
on one condition.

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:15.090
They join his new German Empire.

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:18.411
Once he had their agreements, Bismarck set
out to crush the French.

00:18:18.411 --> 00:18:22.289
The French had gone to war expecting the other
European powers to join them.

00:18:22.289 --> 00:18:26.340
But Austria was now on good terms with Prussia
over that whole “not invading Vienna”

00:18:26.340 --> 00:18:30.730
thing, while the Italians loved Prussia for
giving Austria a kicking.

00:18:30.730 --> 00:18:35.269
Britain and Russia, meanwhile, simply didn’t
care about a squabble between Paris and Berlin.

00:18:35.269 --> 00:18:38.800
So when the threadbare French Army finally
attacked the well-trained Prussian one, on

00:18:38.800 --> 00:18:42.090
August 4, 1870, it wasn’t even a rout.

00:18:42.090 --> 00:18:43.090
It was a massacre.

00:18:43.090 --> 00:18:46.169
Over the next month, the Prussians humiliated
the French.

00:18:46.169 --> 00:18:47.269
First they scattered their army.

00:18:47.269 --> 00:18:48.679
Then they invaded France.

00:18:48.679 --> 00:18:50.610
Then they laid siege to Paris.

00:18:50.610 --> 00:18:55.580
Finally, on September 2, 1870, the Prussians
surrounded and captured Napoleon III.

00:18:55.580 --> 00:18:58.100
It was the death blow for the Second French
Empire.

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:01.210
It was also the birth of the German one.

00:19:01.210 --> 00:19:07.129
On January 18, 1871, Bismarck and Wilhelm
traveled to Louis XIV’s old palace of Versailles

00:19:07.129 --> 00:19:09.570
to declare the creation of the German Empire.

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:10.570
It was touch and go.

00:19:10.570 --> 00:19:14.950
At the last moment, Wilhelm decided he didn’t
want to be Emperor but king, and it was only

00:19:14.950 --> 00:19:19.210
after another multi-hour screaming match that
Bismarck convinced Wilhelm that if you don’t

00:19:19.210 --> 00:19:23.700
do this now, dum-dum, everything we’ve been
working for this last decade will be lost!

00:19:23.700 --> 00:19:25.720
Finally, though, the empire was declared.

00:19:25.720 --> 00:19:30.940
Beneath a ceiling fresco of Louis XIV invading
the Holy Roman Empire, French representatives

00:19:30.940 --> 00:19:35.730
surrendered the territory of Alsace-Lorraine
to Germany, and agreed to pay an indemnity

00:19:35.730 --> 00:19:37.510
of five billion francs.

00:19:37.510 --> 00:19:42.100
Not 20 kilometers away, Prussian bombs continued
to fall onto the citizens of Paris, leaving

00:19:42.100 --> 00:19:44.750
them shell shocked in more ways than one.

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:49.940
Almost a quarter of a century after the Liberal
revolutionaries of 1848 had failed to do so

00:19:49.940 --> 00:19:55.169
with speeches, Bismarck had finally created
Germany with blood and iron.

00:19:55.169 --> 00:20:03.299
It was the dawning of a brave new world, and
Europe would never be the same again.

00:20:03.299 --> 00:20:10.840
Germany: Fatherland
If the 1860s were Bismarck’s decade of war,

00:20:10.840 --> 00:20:13.090
the 1870s and ‘80s were his decades of peace.

00:20:13.090 --> 00:20:16.690
With France crippled and Germany suddenly
a thing, the other powers sat at the table

00:20:16.690 --> 00:20:21.649
all started exchanging uneasy looks with one
another, wondering if this new beer swilling

00:20:21.649 --> 00:20:23.990
nation was gonna start something.

00:20:23.990 --> 00:20:25.399
But they needn’t have worried.

00:20:25.399 --> 00:20:31.059
Unlike Kasier Wilhelm II, or Hitler, or Napoleon,
Bismarck was a conqueror who believed in restraint.

00:20:31.059 --> 00:20:34.230
With the Franco-Prussian War over, he halted
German expansion.

00:20:34.230 --> 00:20:36.559
Turned his attention to internal security.

00:20:36.559 --> 00:20:40.900
While future generations would assume Germany’s
existence was a matter of destiny, Bismarck

00:20:40.900 --> 00:20:43.549
knew how fragile his new empire was.

00:20:43.549 --> 00:20:47.059
Instead of fighting, now was the time for
ruling.

00:20:47.059 --> 00:20:50.879
As dual minister-president of Prussia and
Chancellor of the new Germany, Bismarck ruled

00:20:50.879 --> 00:20:54.639
with a strange combination of enlightenment
and repression.

00:20:54.639 --> 00:20:58.049
On the enlightened side, he introduced universal
male suffrage.

00:20:58.049 --> 00:21:00.629
Established a national healthcare system.

00:21:00.629 --> 00:21:02.279
Introduced accident insurance.

00:21:02.279 --> 00:21:03.759
Brought in old age pensions.

00:21:03.759 --> 00:21:08.070
This was Europe’s very first modern welfare
state, and it was complemented by enlightened

00:21:08.070 --> 00:21:10.049
acts on the world stage.

00:21:10.049 --> 00:21:13.679
In 1885, Bismarck hosted a great power conference
that ended the scramble for Africa.

00:21:13.679 --> 00:21:17.710
He also devoted himself to European peace,
going out of his way to accommodate Russia,

00:21:17.710 --> 00:21:20.330
England, Austria, Italy… even France.

00:21:20.330 --> 00:21:24.610
But behind this enlightened statesman lay
a guy who just couldn’t stop himself from

00:21:24.610 --> 00:21:27.220
occasionally acting like a petulant dictator.

00:21:27.220 --> 00:21:31.549
In the early 1870s, for example, Bismarck
launched a policy known as Kulturkampf.

00:21:31.549 --> 00:21:36.200
Dressed up – as these things usually are
– as a way of preserving ‘native culture’,

00:21:36.200 --> 00:21:40.290
it was really just an excuse to crack down
on German Catholics and try and turn them

00:21:40.290 --> 00:21:42.299
into good, Prussian-style Protestants.

00:21:42.299 --> 00:21:46.500
This was followed, in turn, by a crack down
on socialists and social democrats, and anyone

00:21:46.500 --> 00:21:51.110
basically to the left of Bismarck, which was
basically everyone under 50.

00:21:51.110 --> 00:21:55.139
Bismarck was terrified of exiting in his own
revolution.

00:21:55.139 --> 00:21:59.230
So he assumed cracking down on the forces
he feared would keep him in office forever.

00:21:59.230 --> 00:22:03.190
But while Bismarck was never overthrown, he
couldn’t keep his enemies out of government,

00:22:03.190 --> 00:22:04.190
either.

00:22:04.190 --> 00:22:08.220
When the election of 1890 rolled around, two
decades of anti-Catholic, anti-socialist policies

00:22:08.220 --> 00:22:11.299
resulted in a landslide for the anti-Bismarck
forces.

00:22:11.299 --> 00:22:16.309
However, it was 1888 rather than 1890 that
proved to be Bismarck’s undoing.

00:22:16.309 --> 00:22:20.679
In German history, 1888 is known as The Year
of the Three Emperors.

00:22:20.679 --> 00:22:25.690
That’s because, on March 9, Wilhelm I passed
away, and Fredrick III became Emperor.

00:22:25.690 --> 00:22:29.669
Bismarck had known for years that Wilhelm
was on the way out, and had spent all that

00:22:29.669 --> 00:22:33.179
time grooming Frederick just as he had Frederick’s
father.

00:22:33.179 --> 00:22:37.279
Unfortunately for Bismarck’s well-laid plans,
Frederick III lasted just 99 days in office

00:22:37.279 --> 00:22:38.749
before dying himself.

00:22:38.749 --> 00:22:40.970
In his wake, another man rose to the imperial
throne.

00:22:40.970 --> 00:22:46.450
A man who would go down in history for the
wrong reasons: Kaiser Wilhelm II.

00:22:46.450 --> 00:22:49.690
Wilhelm II was everything Bismarck couldn’t
stand.

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:54.460
Impulsive, sulky, prone to absurd decisions
and, most importantly, utterly unwilling to

00:22:54.460 --> 00:22:59.940
listen to a stuffy old windbag who’d served
his grandfather and great-grandfather.

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:04.830
Barely had Wilhelm II’s backside warmed
the imperial throne than he publicly dismissed

00:23:04.830 --> 00:23:06.870
Bismarck in 1890.

00:23:06.870 --> 00:23:10.879
The Iron Chancellor returned to his old estate
in a huff, expecting the government to recall

00:23:10.879 --> 00:23:12.289
him at any moment.

00:23:12.289 --> 00:23:13.289
But they never did.

00:23:13.289 --> 00:23:17.919
Although he lived 8 more years, Bismarck would
never be in a position of power again.

00:23:17.919 --> 00:23:25.700
Death of a Blacksmith
If Bismarck’s life had been the great roar

00:23:25.700 --> 00:23:29.450
of an iron ox, his retirement was the bellowing
of a wounded bull.

00:23:29.450 --> 00:23:34.659
Until his death, Bismarck wrote books and
pamphlets and made speeches lionizing himself

00:23:34.659 --> 00:23:39.419
while tarring Wilhelm II and his government
as a bunch of incompetent boobs.

00:23:39.419 --> 00:23:44.409
While this was doubtless cheering to his supporters,
it laid an unfortunate precedent.

00:23:44.409 --> 00:23:49.559
Bismarck’s self-mythologizing rants cemented
the image of Bismarck the blacksmith, single-handedly

00:23:49.559 --> 00:23:51.480
forging Germany’s destiny.

00:23:51.480 --> 00:23:56.549
It was an idea that hung around in German
politics into the mid-20th Century, influencing

00:23:56.549 --> 00:23:59.820
another self-proclaimed man of destiny: Adolf
Hitler.

00:23:59.820 --> 00:24:04.080
Unfortunate, too, was the way Bismarck simply
ranted, rather than teach.

00:24:04.080 --> 00:24:08.009
In his life, he’d created a complex system
of alliances and friendship to ensure no other

00:24:08.009 --> 00:24:09.759
power tried to attack Germany.

00:24:09.759 --> 00:24:13.269
But the system was so complex that no-one
else could really grasp it.

00:24:13.269 --> 00:24:18.409
And so his departure marked the beginning
of the slippery slope to WWI.

00:24:18.409 --> 00:24:22.159
Finally, on July 30, 1898, Bismarck passed
away.

00:24:22.159 --> 00:24:26.380
In his last moments, he expressed regret that
he hadn’t been kinder to his dog, Sultan,

00:24:26.380 --> 00:24:29.129
before saying that he’d soon see his wife
Johanna again.

00:24:29.129 --> 00:24:30.629
He was 83.

00:24:30.629 --> 00:24:34.070
From our modern perspective, it’s impossible
to overstate how important Bismarck was.

00:24:34.070 --> 00:24:39.139
He managed to take a group of disparate European
states and transform them into a single, united

00:24:39.139 --> 00:24:40.139
whole.

00:24:40.139 --> 00:24:44.509
It’s telling that, even in the wake of WWI
and then WWII, no-one ever seriously considered

00:24:44.509 --> 00:24:46.320
breaking Germany back up again.

00:24:46.320 --> 00:24:51.749
Even the creation of Communist East Germany
ended with the two Germanys reuniting in 1990.

00:24:51.749 --> 00:24:55.240
Modern Germany may be smaller than it was
in Bismarck’s day, but it’s continued

00:24:55.240 --> 00:24:58.260
existence is now a geopolitical given.

00:24:58.260 --> 00:25:00.210
Can we imagine a world without Bismarck?

00:25:00.210 --> 00:25:03.019
Certainly, it would look very different from
our world today.

00:25:03.019 --> 00:25:08.029
Berlin would be a backwater, the European
Union likely wouldn’t exist, and Germany

00:25:08.029 --> 00:25:13.409
would just be a crazy dream some wayward Liberals
had once, back in 1848.

00:25:13.409 --> 00:25:15.820
On the other hand, we’d have had no WWI.

00:25:15.820 --> 00:25:16.820
No WWII.

00:25:16.820 --> 00:25:19.070
No Hitler, or Goebbels, or the Final Solution.

00:25:19.070 --> 00:25:21.049
Would it be a better world?

00:25:21.049 --> 00:25:24.179
This far removed, all we can say is that it’d
be unrecognizable.

00:25:24.179 --> 00:25:29.070
Those who followed Bismarck may have used
his creation for evil ends, but there’s

00:25:29.070 --> 00:25:32.450
no doubting the Iron Chancellor himself was
a great man of history.

00:25:32.450 --> 00:25:36.409
Bismarck once said he wanted to make his own
music on the world stage.

00:25:36.409 --> 00:25:56.070
Even now, over 120 years after his death,
we’re still all singing from his hymn sheet.

